  <div>Since 2014, the online encyclopedia known as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org">Wikipedia</a> has required <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Reliable_sources">secondary sources</a> in order to cite a news article. This is a useful heuristic for sorting <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Notability">notable</a> events from the incorrigible cacophony of innumerable happenings (including those which fail to happen) produced by the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth_Estate">news media</a> of the twenty-first century.

<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queer_Vox">Queer Vox</a> is a queer news organisation which does not (yet) have a Wikipedia article due to its lack of notability. It is not mentioned in any <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Reliable_sources">reliable secondary sources</a>, nor is it mentioned on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamamia_Podcasts">the Wikipedia article for Mamamia</a> (the network which produces the weekly podcast which airs under the <i>Queer Vox</i> name).

My name is Daphne Preston-Kendal. I am a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonbinary">nonbinary</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transsexual">transsexual</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lesbian">lesbian</a>. My page on Wikipedia was <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Articles_for_deletion/Daphne_Preston-Kendal">deleted</a> in 2015 due to my lack of notability. Despite my vanishingly microscopic presence in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blogosphere">blogosphere</a> (<a href="https://daphne.gay">blog</a>), <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mastodon_(social_network)">Fediverse</a> (<a href="https://glitch.social/@daphne">@daphne@glitch.social</a>), and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unicode">Unicode</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technical_standard">standard</a> (<a href="https://daphne.gay/emoji">legacy</a>), I am, like Queer Vox, not mentioned in any reliable secondary sources.

In October of 2024, I was interviewed for an episode of the Queer Vox news program. While the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronological_dating">chronological date</a> of my interview is unknown (due to the editorial process requiring a turn-around time of multiple weeks), it was <i>published</i> on November 1, 2024.

